id: rust.backpressure
kind: rust
title: backpressure over tokio channels and semaphores
aliases: backpressure, bounded channel, semaphore, backpressure propagation
tags: async, tokio, resilience, concurrency
questions:
  - how do you stop producers when consumers fall behind?
  - where does backpressure travel in an async pipeline?
invariant: Backpressure propagates upstream only when a downstream buffer is full, and only then; an unbounded buffer makes it disappear as a memory leak instead.
answer_30s: Use a bounded tokio::sync::mpsc or a tokio::sync::Semaphore as the choke point: a full send or a borrowed permit blocks the producer, which is the signal the upstream stage can shed load or shed work.
when_to_use:
  - any multi-stage async pipeline where one stage is slower than the previous
  - limiting concurrency into a downstream service
failure_modes:
  - an unbounded channel that swallows backpressure and grows memory without signalling
  - a semaphore that is too large so backpressure never reaches the source
related:
  - design.backpressure
  - rust.tokio-mpsc
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/tokio/latest/tokio/sync/struct.Semaphore.html
